Make FakeNet-NG v3.5 survive `DivertTraffic: No`.  DRAFT-057 Phase 3.

Pinned to upstream tag v3.5, read 2026-09-01.  Reference copy: nothing in this
repository deploys it, because the development container is not Dom0.  The
runbook that applies it is `docs/runbooks/13-verify-egress-modes.md`, step 6.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============

`automation/fakenet.ini` sets `DivertTraffic: No`, and it has to -- FakeNet's
own diverter installs a BLANKET `PREROUTING` REDIRECT that would answer every
analysis VLAN at once, including the clones submitted as `offline`.  Per-job
egress selection is the whole of DRAFT-057 and it cannot survive a process that
redirects everything.  The ini's header carries the long version.

Upstream does not appear to run that path.  With diversion disabled,
`Fakenet.start()` leaves `self.diverter` at `None` (fakenet.py:49, assigned
only inside the `diverttraffic == 'yes'` branch) and three call sites
dereference it with no guard:

  1. fakenet.py, `start()`             -- `fn_addr` is bound at line 158,
     inside that same branch, and read at line 217 outside it.  FakeNet dies
     with `UnboundLocalError` before any listener binds.
     Reported upstream: https://github.com/mandiant/flare-fakenet-ng/issues/167

  2. ProxyListener.py, `get_top_listener()` -- calls
     `diverter.getOriginalDestPort()` before the taste loop, in a handler with
     no enclosing `try`.  Every proxied connection is accepted and then closed
     with no data, and a traceback goes to stderr.

  3. diverterbase.py, `DiverterListenerCallbacks` -- constructed with `None` at
     fakenet.py:211 and forwarding straight through.  All eight listeners
     (HTTP, DNS, FTP, Raw, SMTP, POP, IRC, TFTP) call `logNbi()` on every
     transaction, and `DNSListener.parse()` calls `isProcessBlackListed()` as
     its first statement, before the query is decoded.

(2) and (3) are what make this more than a startup bug: without them the
responder starts, binds its ports, logs nothing wrong, and answers nothing --
which reads in a report as a sample whose infrastructure was down.  That is the
confident false negative DRAFT-057 exists to prevent, so it is not acceptable
to run `simulated` without this patch.

Hunk (3) is the load-bearing one.  Guarding the callbacks object rather than
its six call sites is deliberate: it fixes DNS and all eight listeners' NBI
logging at once, and it keeps the two `mapProxySportToOrigSport()` calls in
ProxyListener untouched, so the diff against upstream stays at three hunks.

WHAT IT COSTS
=============

`dport` becomes `None` inside `taste()`.  Every listener treats it as a bonus
(`confidence = 1 if dport == 53 else 0`, and so on) and adds +2 on a content
match, so protocol detection still works -- it is content-only.  Two
consequences the ini's listener ordering accounts for:

  * FTPListener scores +1 on a content match, the same flat 1 RawListener
    returns for anything.  `get_top_listener` breaks ties by first-listed, so
    the protocol listeners must precede the Raw pair in the ini.
  * The original destination port is genuinely unrecoverable here.  For TCP it
    is still in the kernel (`SO_ORIGINAL_DST`), but reading it is upstream
    feature work, not a guard, and it is deliberately not attempted below.

APPLYING IT
===========

Inside the venv FakeNet was installed into, from the repository root:

    patch -p1 --forward -d "$(python -c 'import fakenet, os; \
        print(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(fakenet.__file__)))')" \
        < automation/fakenet-divert-no.patch

`--forward` makes a re-run say "previously applied" instead of asking.  Re-run
it after any `pip install --upgrade`, and re-check it against upstream before
moving off v3.5: hunk (3) is in a class upstream added recently and is the most
likely of the three to be fixed or restructured there.

diff -u -r a/fakenet/diverters/diverterbase.py b/fakenet/diverters/diverterbase.py
--- a/fakenet/diverters/diverterbase.py
+++ b/fakenet/diverters/diverterbase.py
@@ -2055,7 +2055,15 @@
 
         This method forwards the provided NBI information to the logNbi() method
         in the underlying diverter object. Called by all listeners to log NBIs.
+
+        A no-op under `DivertTraffic: No`, where there is no diverter to
+        delegate to. Every listener calls this on every transaction, so without
+        the guard the first fake DNS answer or HTTP response is replaced by an
+        AttributeError -- caught and logged by some listeners, fatal to the
+        request in all of them.
         """
+        if self.__diverter is None:
+            return
         self.__diverter.logNbi(sport, nbi, proto, application_layer_proto,
                                is_ssl_encrypted)
 
@@ -2068,11 +2076,25 @@
         mapProxySportToOrigSport() method in the underlying diverter object.
         Called by ProxyListener to report the mapping between proxy initiated
         source port and original source port.
+
+        A no-op under `DivertTraffic: No`: the mapping exists so the diverter
+        can attribute a proxied connection back to the process that made it,
+        and with no diverter there is nothing holding the table.
         """
+        if self.__diverter is None:
+            return
         self.__diverter.mapProxySportToOrigSport(proto, orig_sport, proxy_sport,
                                                  is_ssl_encrypted)
 
     def isProcessBlackListed(self, proto, sport):
         """Check if the process is blacklisted.
+
+        `(False, None, None)` under `DivertTraffic: No` -- nothing is
+        blacklisted, and the process behind a connection is unknowable without
+        the diverter's conntrack bookkeeping. DNSListener.parse() calls this
+        before it decodes the query, so an unguarded None here means the
+        responder answers nothing at all.
         """
+        if self.__diverter is None:
+            return False, None, None
         return self.__diverter.isProcessBlackListed(proto, sport=sport)
